  "title": "The main line: 3.Nc3 and 3.Nd2",
  "defaultTags": ["development"],
  "intro": "White defends the e-pawn a second time and asks you to make the first decision. You take: 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4, and now the bishop comes out to f5.\n\n## Why 3...dxe4\n\nLeaving the pawn on d5 hands White the choice between e5 and exd5 at the moment that suits White best. Taking removes that choice and costs White a move to recapture. It also opens the b1-h7 diagonal for your bishop before ...e6 ever has to be played, which is the whole justification for the Caro-Kann.\n\n## The long forced sequence\n\nAfter 4...Bf5 5.Ng3 Bg6 6.h4 h6 7.Nf3 Nd7 8.h5 Bh7 9.Bd3 Bxd3 10.Qxd3 e6 the game reaches a position both sides have been steering towards since move four. It looks like a lot of moves to remember. It is really one idea repeated: White gains time on the bishop, the bishop keeps stepping to the only square that works, and the sequence ends with the bishop traded for White's best attacker.\n\n## What you have when it ends\n\nA sound structure, no weaknesses, and one plan: finish developing, castle, then play ...c5 against d4. White is usually castled queenside and pushing on the kingside, so opening the c-file points straight at White's king. Space belongs to White; the clearer plan belongs to you.\n\n## 3.Nd2\n\nThe same thing by another route. After 3...dxe4 4.Nxe4 the knight has arrived from d2 instead of c3 and the position is identical, so play 4...Bf5 and follow this chapter.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nPlaying ...Bf5 on move three, before the exchange on e4, when 4.exd5 cxd5 5.Qb3 hits b7 and d5 at once. Putting the knight on f6 before d7, which invites Ne5. Grabbing at h5 with ...h5 instead of ...h6, which leaves a hole on g5. And, later, forgetting ...c5 and drifting into a worse ending.",
